ng five groups of 25  different kids you know they have a  class of 30 kids for math and science  and 30 kids for math and science and  they stay with those kids through the  day I mean the problem is that education  is so centralized and even simple tweaks  on the margin you can't do in a school  district so on the one hand charter  schools just have a lot of freedom to do  kind of common-sense changes that we  would all expect and to really monitor  their performance and so their  relationships with teachers are much  different so in most districts teachers  are assigned schools in the same way  that kids are assigned to schools you  know it's based on seniority or where  there's a need and there's not you know  the principal may not have even the  chance to interview the teacher to see  if they're a good fit for the school so  on the front end it's kind of a mutual  relationship where i'm a teacher and you  interview me and i want to work at your  school and we're all on the same team to  make this school better and so there is  kind of a different personnel  relationship and an ownership of the  outcomes for the kids because everyone  is there through voluntary  and that's a positive thing and so I  think that explains a lot of the  difference right there and then the  other thing is you are starting to see  more and more experimentation in charter  schools where they actually do say okay  I want to have a niche curriculum where  I'm going to have you know a tag-along  immersion school where kids you know  learn to all of their lessons in two  different languages and if that works  for the kids and the parents that's  great or we're going to focus on stem  and math and science and technology and  that's going to be the focus of our  school and you know I think one of the  things that's different is that they can  spend so much more time experimenting  and measuring the results without having  to spend so much time being accountable  for paperwork and I know that