isconnected from the student because of  charter schools and these voucher  programs people have been much more  familiar the way that we fund higher  education whether it's a Pell Grant or a  student loan you know they get paid  based on a student enrolling who pays  tuition and private schools get tuition  based on the kids that enroll but in the  public space it's really been a  disconnect between the money and the  kids and so I think that's really  starting to change is that we're  starting to think about and especially  because of the digital revolution  moving into so an interesting program in  Louisiana is called course choice and so  Governor Jindal New Orleans has almost  eighty percent of kids or higher in  charter schools and then they have a  statewide voucher program where kids can  go to private schools or public schools  and so the governor the next thing that  he decided to do was to go one step  further where not only will we have a  voucher attached to kids but we should  be able to have multiple providers so if  you want to take a physics course from  this provider and you want to do  physical education at karate class and  you know that you should actually be  able to fund the student down to the  incremental level of the course choice  not just one voucher that would go to  one provider so we're actually starting  to see trends in Arizona has a similar  program called education savings  accounts where the parents actually  don't have to take a private a voucher  to one school they would actually get  the value of the per pupil dollars and  then they would put that in a savings  account similar to like a medical  savings account and then they could use  that money for any kind of educational  services that they wanted to get for  their kid so you know if they needed a  speech therapist or they wanted to have  you know some kind of special science  camp that they sent their kid to all in  adapting sounds it sounds expensive but  in reality it would be driving costs  down as a whole right right so it's  interesting i was talking the other day  to the sky from the broad foundation who  does a lot of investment in education  and he was talking about New Jersey in  Newark right the kids are funded at  twenty-seven thousand dollars a kid per  year and if you just didn't have those  kids go to school and you put that  twenty seven thousand dollars into an  investment account every year and let it  compound when they were eighteen they  would be set for life financially I mean  we are already spending like a huge huge  amount of money on these kids and we  can't see what the value is that we get  for that money and you brought up  Louisiana and New Orleans and yeah they  have they do have a lot of programs and  they've gotten praise but they've also  gotten criticism so can you talk  about this and whether it can and still  would be a good model for other parts of  the country to replicate right so if we  start with describing what's happened in  New Orleans after Hurricane Katrina  basically the public school system was  wiped out and the traditional schools  were disbanded and the unions the  contract was null and void and so they  really had a chance to have a fresh  start and so in New Orleans almost a  hundred percent of the kids will be  going they all go to choice space  schools and eighty percent of them are  independent operators that have schools  and so in the last five years New  Orleans has seen a tremendous amount of  growth in student achievement graduation  rates parental satisfaction and that's  the thing is there's both quantitative  and qualitative measures that we care  about I mean obviously we want to know  that we're getting some kind of basic  performance in terms of student  achievement for the money we're spending  but then the other thing is is that  because of the tragedy of the Commons so  many public schools don't even do  fundamental things like clean bathrooms  you know food that's edible safety for  the children and you know it's harder to  quantify those things in places like New  Orleans but that's one of the big  indicators is the parents are happy and  satisfied because the kids are in clean  you know positive environments where  they're safe and you know so the critics  you know say the best that they can do  is say oh these claims are exaggerated  things aren't as good as they say they  are but clearly on multiple indicators  everything has moved in a positive  direction and the other thing is other  districts so Jefferson Parish which is  close by New Orleans has actually  started copying and opening up their  lower performing schools to charter  schools and they implemented a money  follow the child's school finance system  so you know I think there's a new book  out by this guy called Andy smack where  he basically makes the argument that the  school district should be disbanded and  that we should have contractual  relationships with individual schools or  groups of schools and that that's really  where  are moving because we can hold them  accountable when we do that great and  for the actual curricula is education  how education is being taught are being  changed with the charter schools and  other forms of choice schools or is it  just moving the kids to a better  facility and taking conventional  education and just improving it right so  I mean it's both at the one hand there's  a lot of change kind of just at the  margins where you know in a lot of cases  these schools are traditional college  prep you know they say that their  mission is to make kids more prepared  for college and they still are teaching  you know the same curriculum the same  kind of standardized you know it's not  that the schools look revolutionary but  they do things so much better in that  there's more communication between the  teachers it may be something as simple  as changing the scheduling so that a  math and science teacher only has  instead of having five groups of 25  different kids you know they have a  class of 30 kids for math and science  and 30 kids for math and science and  they stay with those kids through the  day I mean the problem is that education  is so centralized and even simple tweaks  on the margin you can't do in a school  district so on the one hand charter  schools just have a lot of freedom to do  kind of common-sense changes that we  would all expect and to really monitor  their performance and so their  relationships with teachers are much  different so in most districts teachers  are assigned schools in the same way  that kids are assigned to schools you  know it's based on seniority or where  there's a need and there's not you know  the principal may not have even the  chance to interview the teacher to see  if they're a good fit for the school so  on the front end it's kind of a mutual  relationship where i'm a teacher and you  interview me and i want to work at your  school and we're all on the same team to  make this school better and so there is  kind of a different personnel  relationship and an ownership of the  outcomes for the kids because everyone  is there through voluntary  and that's a positive thing and so I  think that explains a lot of the  difference right there and then the  other thing is you are starting to see  more and more experimentation in charter  schools where they actually do say okay  I want to have a niche curriculum where  I'm going to have you know a tag-along  immersion school where kids you know  learn to all of their lessons in two  different languages and if that works  for the kids and the parents that's  great or we're going to focus on stem  and math and science and technology and  that's going to be the focus of our  school and you know I think one of the  things that's different is that they can  spend so much more time experimenting  and measuring the results without having  to spend so much time being accountable  for paperwork and I know that charter  schools do boast better results but i'm  wondering i mean critics might think  that because charter schools are taking  skimming the top of the class anyway  what's saying that it's not just those  kids who would be doing better anyway  and what happens to the kids who are  left behind time so i mean there's been  a lot of focus on the demographics of  charter schools and a large number of  these high-performing charter schools so  for example here in los angeles they  have the exact same demographics as the  traditional public schools because most  charter schools are oversubscribed and  that means they have waiting lists and  so that means they truly have random  assignment when it comes to who gets  into their charter school because they  have to hold lotteries and lots of kids  don't get in but they're the same large  population of kids that want to go to  that school and so I'm not saying that  there's never been a case of a charter  school skimming obviously I'm sure there  has been but in general these charter  schools that are high performing are  serving a very similar population and in  many cases they serve kids that have  been ill served by the traditional  public schools and they actually may be  at an even lower quartile in terms of  reading and math achievement and things  like that when they start out in the  charter school because they've left the  traditional public school because they  aren't learning  these kind of basic student achievement  areas so take eva moskowitz is a very  outspoken charter school proponent in  new york and she has a group of schools  called Success Academy and one of the  interesting features about her school is  they actually share a traditional public  school with a group of kids that go to  the traditional public school and the  kids in the charter school are far  outscoring the kids in the same  classrooms in the same buildings in the  traditional public school and would you  think that those teachers or  administrators would ever like walk  across the hall your geez you're getting  20 points higher is there something that  you're doing different than us and  that's the thing as education they're so  siloed and unwilling to learn from some  of these best practices that these other  schools you doing that is is it is it  teachers unions I mean I think that the  only way that you can incentivize people  to improve their behavior is if there is  some kind of change when they do badly  so you know if I don't do my job I lose  my job but in most traditional public  schools so take Los Angeles again for an  example a school that's out forty  percent capacity now because most of  their student body has gone to a charter  school or another choice in Los Angeles  still has a fully funded teachers  curriculum facility and they've seen  really no change on the ground because  they've lost so many kids or because  they're low scoring so until the money  actually follows the child and you  actually and so a bunch of urban  districts have tried this now so let's  take Baltimore for example because I  think it's a really compelling example  you know Baltimore was made famous for  having terrible schools because of the  wire right season four really  highlighted you know and kids weren't  going to class doing drugs crime  whatever and so Baltimore five years ago  got a new superintendent that insisted  that as part of his contract he would  have autonomy to do what he wanted and  one of the things he did is he has been  very open to charters  schools coming into Baltimore and  running low performing schools but then  the other thing that he did is he said  every school will be a school of choice  and the money within the district will  follow the child and schools that have  low performance and low enrollment will  be closed and so he actually looked at  the data and a lot of schools got closed  they turned over forty percent of the  principles that could not manage their  budgets and so Baltimore today has on  almost every indicator higher test  scores higher graduation rates but some  of the most compelling data out of  Baltimore is actually not traditional  school performance but data that affects  the community and that is that  incarceration for high school students  has fallen by sixty percent murder and  homicide rates has fallen by seventy two  percent for disadvantaged kids that go  to those urban city schools in Baltimore  so in some ways if you were writing the  script for the wire today many of the  things that used to happen to those kids  in terms of outcomes and the primary  reason for that is you know there are a  lot of teachers that are working hard  there's a lot of principles that are  working hard but if those high schools  don't keep those kids interested in  school and if they just let them leave  they don't get the money and that's the  fundamental thing that has changed in  Baltimore is if the kids aren't there  and the teachers aren't there there's no  money for that individual school and  let's talk about race and class a little  bit more I mean you brought up  demographics in student bodies but what  about i mean the proponents of school  choice are mainly upper-class  republicans and working class blacks and  you don't usually see these groups you  know in the same room working together  so i mean it's interesting i think more  and more we are seeing a lot of kind of  bipartisan support so the Brookings  Institute right which is a very kind of  progressive kind of traditional liberal  think tank they actually now have  started what they call a choice and  competition index and they actually rank  every traditional school district in the  United States on how much choice and  competition that they have and for them  to take such an investment and that kind  of an initiative and so Louisiana New  Orleans actually ranks number one and  surprisingly New York City ranks second  and they look at things like how many  alternatives exist does the funding  follow the child is there information  for the parents to make a correct choice  but so when you have groups like the  Brookings Institute that have made one  of their fundamental education  initiatives measuring choice and  competition you can see how far we've  come and so the Los Angeles school  choice event that just happened here on  febuary on January 25th they actually  had a group of charter schools and  speakers with a lot of kids and Gloria  Romero who used to be a state center  here in Los Angeles and is a you know  classic Democrat she actually runs up  Democrats for education reform and you  know you see more and more people that  are substantial leaders in the  Democratic Party so whether it's cory  booker or someone like Gloria Romero  they're coming out for school choice as  a civil rights issue and then I think  that's kind of strange bedfellows or a  strange coalition with kind of the more  people that have always made arguments  for choice from kind of an economic  perspective or a market people deserve  choice versus the civil rights issue and  you see these groups working together in  positive ways to get bills passed so for  instance Florida which has been a hotbed  of school choice you know Jeb Bush was  able to work with the black Democratic  caucus to get school choice passed in  that state and so you really see that  happening more and more across the  country is it is these coalition's from  both sides of the aisle now does that  mean that there's not a position from  the status quo or the unions or the  establishment they still spend a lot of  money to oppose these things but more  and more people are speaking out and the  other thing is we have a lot of examples  now like Louisiana or like new york city  of you know the world doesn't come to an  end when we have school choice right and  opponents on other side might say school  choices and going far enough we want  complete separation between schools and  state we shouldn't have public schools  at all so we really have a continuum of  school choice you know if you think  about the ultimate market in education  parents would take full responsibility  either through homeschooling or private  paying for education and then on this  end we have traditionally funded  ninety-nine percent of the money goes to  public schools and it's all based on  taxpayer money and so in between that we  have a lot of steps on how close we are  to a true free market and people get  pretty upset about where they come down  on that continuum you know people who  like tax credits they like them better  than vouchers because when you get to  keep your own money you have less  government entanglement but we're still  at a place where we just are starting to  make inroads into competition at the  margins and the fact is we have a single  provider and a single payer system and  so in my view and I think in this kind  of bigger tent approach anything that  moves us along to breaking up that  education monopoly where the money  follows the child is an improvement and  I can understand the risk that we don't  want private schools to become heavily  regulated you know we don't want choices  to be diminished because private  enterprise starts mimicking government  schools and I think while that is  something we have to be vigilant about  and is a real caution if you look at  purely private schools today that take  no government money they're still  regulated in many many ways by the  government whether it's how far the  swingset can be from the parking lot  health and safety food safety you know  they are inspected by all kinds of  outside agencies and groups they're  licensed I mean it's not as if we have  this market already where the government  is an intervening so we need to work on  decentralization versus centralization  yeah  it is worth thinking about this is a  battle between centralization and  decentralization I mean the Obama  administration has really been a strong  on what did they call it the race to the  top initiative and the Department of  Education is pushing for national  standards so is this is this hurting  kids right so we have what we call now  the Common Core initiative and it's  interesting because the Obama  administration in some ways comes down  on both sides of the fence they have  taken a small portion of the federal  education budget which is several  billion dollars but still compared to  the hundred and fifty billion dollars  plus taking two billion dollars in  saying we'll divide that up among states  that have innovative proposals and we'll  call it race to the top you know while  we raise taxes and spending for  education overall far and above that so  and the Obama administration has been a  friend to charter schools they've  started several charter school  initiatives but on the on the flip side  you know President Obama would not stand  up for the Washington DC voucher program  while his own daughters were going to  one of the private schools that were  part of that DC scholarship program you  know he was never willing and that was  you know a few million dollars a teeny  tiny part of the overall that the  federal government spends on education  so you know overall the Obama  administration has still come down on  the side of centralization and inputs so  one of their big initiatives is that we  will have a common core set of  curriculum for every state and we'll  have basically a set of performance  tests that will measure that curriculum  for every state and so what's happened  with that is that states like  Massachusetts in actually California and  Indiana that have higher standards have  actually lowered their standards to meet  that bar so in California we have one of  the best math standards in the country  and all eighth graders take algebra  earlier than almost anywhere else in the  country and because of that we actually  have seen  huge gains in our math scores for  low-income and regular kids in  California so now we've changed that and  we're not going to do that anymore  because of common core standards and so  you have these tensions between more  centralization and less centralization  and the status quo at the federal level  the state level certainly buys into more  centralization and you know basically  the way the Obama administration have  gotten states to agree is I don't know  you remember the No Child Left Behind  which was you know George Bush's  education initiative it had certain  proficiency requirements so that every  by 2014 a hundred percent of kids were  supposed to be proficient in math and  reading besides the fact that whoever  designed that was not a mathematician  and that's statistically really  impossible the bottom line is now  without going through any kind of  legislation or congressional approval  but by an administrative waiver process  President Obama's administration has  said if you sign on to common core and  national tests we won't hold you  responsible for that old initiative so  they've never revisited the No Child  Left Behind legally it still stands but  they're basically giving every state a  waiver if you do XY and z we won't hold  you accountable for what was in that  previous law and we won't change the law  we'll just administratively do that so  just I mean wrapping up would you say  looking to the future in 2013 or are you  optimistic or are these other things  getting in the way and impeding school  choice I mean I think that school choice  is winning I'm very optimistic i like to  say that a lot and you know already this  year we've seen several large-scale  proposals for school choice so the  governor of ohio just said that he was  going so ohio has the Cleveland school  choice program and it has another  smaller school choice program for kids  stuck in failing schools the governor  has a proposal to make that into a  statewide school voucher program that  just came out this week  Tennessee you have Memphis is actually  replicating New Orleans and the recovery  school district and it seems to be a  very robust model where they want to  basically make every school a charter  school and make the money follow the  child from one school to the other I  think we've barely scratched the surface  with what technology and the digital  revolution is going to mean for you know  how things like Khan Academy and the  popularity of rocketship education where  they basically have saved on labor costs  where they have used computers and  technology for directed lessons for part  of the day and then they have teachers  just give lectures so they basically are  saving about forty percent of what it  costs to run a traditional school in  terms of teachers salaries you have  things like flipped classrooms where the  kids actually listen to a lecture on a  video at home at night instead of doing  homework and then they do all of their  work in the class with the teacher  supervising that work and so all these  interesting models that are just barely  starting and then the other thing things  like homeschooling millions more kids  can be homeschooled now because of  technology because it's less labor  intensive than it used to be and there's  all these kind of open-source resources  and then the other thing is even though  there's a lot of resistance in the  establishment I think one of the most  positive signs that things are changing  is that all kinds of districts are  changing their teacher contract to allow  for more autonomous schools getting rid  of tenure getting rid of seniority you  know you never would have heard of a  district voluntary negotiating for less  rules and less security without the  pressure and competition from charter  schools and other kinds of choice and so  you know there's a district in Arizona  called veil and they actually the whole  district has no textbooks it's all  online they use powerpoints and the  teachers collaborate and for five years  it's been the highest scoring  traditional school district in Arizona  and it's really blowing away the  competition and  public administrators wouldn't  experiment with things like that if they  weren't feeling market pressure from  everywhere so it's a great time to be a  parent it's a great time for kids  because I think more and more you have  just really a lot of choices available  great well I'm going to cut you off  there but thank you again for talking to  us Lisa snow thanks so much choice E and  thank you for watching reason TVs first  live stream interview so keep checking  reason calm and reason TV for upcoming  events and new videos for reason TV and  Tracy Oppenheimer
 

